<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Seminole Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1750 CE – 1907 CE (approx.) · capital Wewoka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A people formed in 18th-century Florida from Muscogee migrants and escaped Africans, whose resistance in three wars made them the 'unconquered' Seminole.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After English raids emptied northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a> of its earlier peoples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> towns, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitchiti">Hitchiti</a>-speakers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creek</a>, moved into the region. From around 1750 the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowkeeper">Cowkeeper</a> led a group to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paynes-prairie">Alachua prairie</a>, and others followed. The Spanish called them cimarrones, wild or runaway people, which became Seminole. Africans escaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a> joined them as allies.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Seminole towns were autonomous, led by chiefs and war leaders and organised in matrilineal clans, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Green Corn</a> ceremony at their centre. They raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> grasslands and farmed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-seminoles">Black Seminoles</a> lived in their own villages, owing tribute and fighting as allies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson's</a> invasion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-seminole-war">First Seminole War</a> of 1817 to 1818 helped push <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to sell Florida.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-seminole-war">Second Seminole War</a> of 1835 to 1842, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osceola">Osceola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micanopy">Micanopy</a>, the Seminole fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> to a costly standstill, but most were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wewoka-oklahoma">Wewoka</a> became their capital. A few hundred stayed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/everglades">Everglades</a>; their descendants form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seminole-tribe-of-florida">Seminole Tribe of Florida</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/seminole-nation.jpg" alt="Seminole Nation" width="320" height="433"><figcaption>Image: State Library and Archives of Florida · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seminole_mother_and_children-_Brighton_Reservation,_Florida_(8443707301).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1750 CE – 1907 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wewoka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cowkeeper, Micanopy, Osceola</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Seminole traditional religion, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mikasuki, Muscogee</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seminole</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=seminole-nation" aria-label="Seminole Nation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2236 907 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Seminole Nation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2556 1163L2576 1147M2556 1163L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2576 1147h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2556 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2556 1163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wewoka. <a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=seminole-nation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
